   In order to support checking of GDT's and Register preservation in the
scripts without having to edit the scripts,  we have added two new command line
parameters to MASTER.BAT/CMD and to MTDOS/MTOS2.


MASTER.BAT/MASTER.CMD

   For the MASTER files,  the default values for GDT usage and Register
preservation are OFF.  Meaning, if MASTER is executing with no parameters or
with only directory names there will be no GDT usage or Register perservation
checking.  If GDT usage or Register preservation is desired,  a GDT or REGS
respectively should be placed before the subdirectory parameters.  If both are
desired they both should be placed before subdirectory parameters in any order.
For example if you want to execute MASTER on subdirectory OPEN and CLOSE with
GDT usage and Register preservation, either of the following could be used:

	 MASTER GDT REGS OPEN CLOSE
    or	 MASTER REGS GDT OPEN CLOSE

The GDT and REGS parameters will affect all scripts executed under the given
MASTER.  This means that the GDT usage and Register preservation are determined
only at the start of MASTER and all scripts executed under this MASTER will use
these values.  In the example above, both Open and Close scripts will be
executed with GDT usage and Register preservation checking ON.	There is no way
to turn either OFF in the middle of the subdirectory parameters.

   The GDT and REGS parameters set two environment variables, gdts and regs
respectively.  These variables are set to NULL initially.  If GDT is found on
the command line in either the first or second parameter position, gdts is set
equal to /g.  The same holds true for REGS except regs is set equal to /r.
These variables whether they are NULL or set equal to a value are placed on the
MTOS2/MTDOS command line the in first and second parameter postions.  Following
is how the command line parameters look for the OPEN directory in MASTER:

	 MTOS2 %regs% %gdts% open001 open

If neither REGS or GDT was found on the command line parameter of MASTER the
following would be executed:

	 MTOS2 open001 open

However, if REGS was on the command line the following would be executed:

	 MTOS2 /r open001 open




MTOS2/MTDOS

   The parameter format for MTOS2/MTDOS is the following:

	 MTOS2/MTDOS [/r|/R|/g|/G] [Script_Name] [Output_Name]

	 where [] means the parameter is optional.

The /r or /R mean that Register preservation should be toggled ON and the /g or
/G mean that GDT usage should be toggled ON.  These parameters are not required.
They can be present by themselves or together in any order.  For example, if you
want to run OPEN001.S with OPEN.LOG as your output file and with both Register
preservation and GDT usage, either of the following could be used:

	 MTOS2 /r /g OPEN001 OPEN
     or  MTOS2 /g /r OPEN001 OPEN

If you only wanted Register preservation ON the following would be the command:

	 MTOS2 /r OPEN001 OPEN

The /r or /g are only valid as the first or second parameters.	If either one is
not present its corresponding value within the Mttool will be toggled OFF.
Also Register preservation and GDT usage can be turned ON by the command line
but the script can turn them off if it so wishes.  Meaning that the command line
values are not unchangeable once you are in the script.  This is important if
MTOS2/MTDOS is executed with GDT usage ON and the script executes an USEGDT
command thinking it will turn GDT usage ON.  Instead, GDT usage will be toggled
OFF by this command.  If GDT usage and/or Register preservation are still ON
when the script is done (which is usually the case), the Mttool will turn them
OFF before exiting.
